00:13:44.000 --> 00:14:27.000
Russell's competing service was called the Pony Express. Riders on horseback would race at full speed for about 10 or 15 miles to relay stations where they would trade out for a rusted horse. This change was supposed to take only two minutes. Horses were to carry no more than 165 pounds, including the rider. If an exhausted horse collapsed on the trail, the rider was to run on foot to the next location with his bag of mail. It was a very expensive endeavor and it didn't last long, about a year and a half, which was okay because by 1861, the transcontinental telegraph would reach California, and rail service would soon follow.
横贯大陆的电报将到达加州，铁路服务也将紧随其后。火车最终将把邮件送到美国各地，不仅是运送，而且成为移动的邮局。事实上，来自邮局的补贴允许铁路系统在全国范围内扩张。火车无法仅靠客运票价维持运营。他们从邮局获得的资金对于帮助他们扩大服务至关重要。几年前，这些邮政补贴对驿站马车也做了同样的事情，第一次世界大战后，邮政将再次为航空业这样做。

